                Digital Honors Four City Year Alumni
            with Leadership Awards at National Conference

MAYNARD, Mass., June 17, 1996 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
presented leadership awards to four City Year program alumni at City 
Year's national conference, held in Boston on June 14.  Digital is 
one of two national founding sponsors of City Year.  

     City Year is a nationally recognized community service youth 
corps that unites young adults ages 17 to 23 from diverse cultural, 
educational and socioeconomic backgrounds for a year of full-time 
community service.  The organization is headquartered in Boston.  

     The Digital Equipment Corporation Leadership Awards were first 
presented in 1995 to honor corps member alumni who have continued 
their dedication to community service, maintained a commitment to 
excellence and provided an inspiration to others through their 
examples.  Each winner received a Digital HiNote notebook computer 
and a Digital printer. 

This year's winners are:

     o Jin-Wook "Jay" C. Kim, formerly of City Year Boston,
       Massachusetts, City Year Columbia, South Carolina and
       currently working at Public Allies in Washington, D.C.;

     o Chad Olcott, serving at City Year San Jose, California; 

     o Maritza Rosario, serving at City Year Chicago, Illinois; and

     o James Willie, formerly of City Year Boston and currently
       working at the National Association of Partners in Education 
       in Alexandria, Virginia.

     "We are pleased to recognize these four outstanding young 
people with Digital Equipment Corporation Leadership Awards," said 
Ilene B. Jacobs, Digital's vice president and treasurer.  "Digital 
believes in investing in the future through its support of programs 
such as City Year that focus on developing leadership and 
achievement in our children and youth.  These four recipients 
exemplify the City Year ideals and they serve as role models for the 
actions that our young people can take toward the betterment of our 
communities." 

